    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d71">

                <pb facs="0181" n="149" xml:id="pg149"/>

                <head>71. Telegram From the Executive Secretary of the National Security Council
                    (Smith) to the President’s Special Assistant for National Security Affairs
                    (Bundy), at Saigon<anchor n="1" xml:id="d71-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1965-02-04T15:31:00-05:00">February 4, 1965,
                            3:31 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>CAP 65030. Saigon for McGeorge Bundy—deliver opening of business. For your
                    information, President reacted sharply to UPI report from Saigon<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d71-f2"/> saying Khanh appeared to be snubbing you by turning down
                    invitation to Friday<anchor n="3" xml:id="d71-f3"/> reception for Vietnamese
                    military leaders at Westmoreland’s home.</p>
                <p>The President told me that assuming this was true, we should start looking hard
                    for someone who could replace Khanh. He would like you to get full rundown on
                    possible replacements. (We did not then have Embtel 2400.)<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d71-f4"/> I listened and then explained how you were handling
                    postponement of call on Khanh (Embtel 2399).<anchor n="5" xml:id="d71-f5"/></p>
                <p>Subject did not come up in President’s press conference.<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d71-f6"/> State is cabling text of President’s comments on Vietnam
                    and on your mission.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d71-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d71-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, International Meetings and
                    Travel File, McGeorge Bundy—Saigon, Vol. I. Top Secret. Repeated to the CIA for
                    McCone. The date and time stamped on the source text indicate that it was
                    received in Saigon on the morning of February 5, although the hour is
                    illegible.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d71-f2" n="2" xml:id="d71-f2-content">
                <p>The text of the UPI bulletin was quoted in telegram 1617 to Saigon, February 5.
                    (Department of State, Central Files, POL 7 US/Bundy)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d71-f3" n="3" xml:id="d71-f3-content">
                <p>February 5.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d71-f4" n="4" xml:id="d71-f4-content">
